// [API Channel As Signal Carrier.note] search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   An API is a signal carrier between software FSMs.  The medium carrying the signal consists of
   processor accesses of values in memory; memory that is visible to this processor on this mesa.

   Memory as a signal carrier or medium is not really different from Ethernet: a MAC is asked to
   transer a value; a value is physically emitted; the value has to travel from the processor
   to the memory; the memory has to completely capture and retain the value for the receiver.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   External to a signal, but within the context a signal exists in, data is used to manage signals.
   A Media Access Controller (MAC) layer process for each channel controls access to any shared
   resource across any set of channel resources.  Resources: memory, bandwidth, priority queues.
   This ranges from framing bits (start/stop RS232), the preamble before an Ethernet Frame starts,
   or the I2C bit-level signal transitions that indicate clocking periods and data flow direction.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The ssTEA API maintains knowledge of active/!active using a "Magic Number" within the data.
   When an Event API is opened, the magic number is created based on a pointer to ssTEA data space.
   - the value of the pointer is one of: pChannelNull, or the address of a data structure in ssTEA.
   - if a pointer is not pChannelNull, the Magic Number maintained by ssA is the bit-flipped value.

   A pointer value that matches an address of an ssA Event Info Descriptor may be a valid channel.
   A Event Descriptor with a MagicNumber equal to the bit-flipped channel value is valid and open.
   The channel serves to direct the activity to the proper MAC and signal carrier for that channel.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   the ssTEA API bases its knowledge of the activation state on the "channel" pointer value.
   the ssTEA API bases its trust of the user's channel data on the magic number being valid.
   When a signal is received, it is valid if it has a valid channel value (EvInfo address),
   AND the channel's magic number value (e.g., binary bit-flip of all bits in EvInfo's address).

   pApiSig->channel = (ss_EvInfoChannel_t) pEvInfo;
   pEvInfo->MagicNumber = MakeMagicNumber (pEvInfo);

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
